consiSt("ky",{"courts":[{"l":"HIGHEST COURT","n":"Supreme Court of Kentucky","d":"Seven justices, one elected from each of seven Supreme Court districts. Hears direct appeals of right in criminal cases with a sentence of death, life, or 20 years or more (RAP 2(A)(1), (D)). Takes civil cases mainly by discretionary review of the Court of Appeals (RAP 44) or by transfer of a pending appeal (RAP 17). Also answers certified questions of Kentucky law (RAP 50) and governs bar admission and lawyer discipline through the Supreme Court Rules (SCR)."},{"l":"INTERMEDIATE","n":"Court of Appeals","d":"Headquartered in Frankfort (SCR 1.030(1)) and decides cases in three-judge panels (RAP 2(G)(1)). Hears appeals of right from circuit court, including family court divisions. Hears discretionary review of circuit court decisions in cases that began in district court (RAP 44(B)(1)). Also handles workers’ compensation petitions (RAP 49) and original writ actions against circuit judges (RAP 60)."},{"l":"TRIAL COURTS","n":"Circuit Court (including Family Court divisions)","d":"The general-jurisdiction trial court, which sits in judicial circuits. The civil filing fee is $188 from July 1, 2026, plus a $20 court technology fee and other fees (CR 3.02(1)). Family courts established under Ky. Const. § 112(6) are divisions of circuit court, and their appeals go to the Court of Appeals (RAP 2(A)(1)). Circuit court also hears appeals from district court (RAP 48)."},{"l":"LIMITED JURISDICTION","n":"District Court (including Small Claims Division)","d":"The limited-jurisdiction trial court. It handles smaller civil cases, probate, guardianship, forcible detainer, and misdemeanors. The civil filing fee is $94, and $38 in small claims, which covers cases of $2,500 or less (CR 3.03(1)). Appeals go to circuit court within 30 days (RAP 48 and RAP 3), but some statutes set shorter windows, such as 10 days for small claims and 7 days for forcible detainer (RAP 48(B)(2))."}],"slapp":[{"t":"Kentucky has an anti-SLAPP statute: KRS 454.460–454.478 (2022)","d":"Kentucky enacted a Uniform Public Expression Protection Act–style statute in 2022 Ky. Acts ch. 233, effective July 14, 2022. It provides a ‘special motion for expedited relief’ to dismiss a cause of action in whole or in part. The statute has no short title, so ‘UPEPA’ is a descriptive label. Cite the sections themselves.","c":"KRS 454.460–454.478; 2022 Ky. Acts ch. 233","v":true},{"t":"Scope: government proceedings and speech on matters of public concern","d":"The statute covers claims based on three kinds of activity: (a) communication in a legislative, executive, judicial, administrative, or other governmental proceeding; (b) communication on an issue under consideration in such a proceeding; and (c) exercise of free speech, press, assembly, petition, or association rights under the U.S. or Kentucky Constitution on a matter of public concern. ‘Matter of public concern’ is defined in KRS 454.460(4).\nIt excludes ten categories (KRS 454.462(2)(a)). These include claims against or by government units in certain capacities, commercial speech about the defendant’s own goods or services, real-property claims, bodily injury, wrongful death, and survival claims, insurance-code and insurance-contract claims, common-law fraud, KRS Chapters 401–407, employment statutes (KRS 337, 338, 339, 342, 344, 345, including whistleblower claims), and the Consumer Protection Act. The statute still applies to news, artistic, and audiovisual work and to consumer reviews and ratings, even within those excluded categories (KRS 454.462(2)(b)).","c":"KRS 454.460(4), 454.462","v":true},{"t":"Deadline: 60 days from service of the pleading","d":"File the special motion no later than 60 days after being served with the complaint, crossclaim, counterclaim, third-party claim, or other pleading asserting the claim. A later filing requires a showing of good cause.","c":"KRS 454.464","v":true},{"t":"Automatic stay of everything between the movant and respondent","d":"Filing the motion stays all other proceedings between the moving and responding parties, including discovery and pending hearings and motions. On motion, the court may also stay matters involving other parties. The stay lasts until the ruling and the expiration of the time to appeal. If anyone appeals, all proceedings between all parties are stayed until the appeal ends.\nThe court must allow limited discovery if a party shows that specific information is necessary to the KRS 454.472(1) burdens and is not reasonably available otherwise. Fee motions, voluntary dismissals, motions to sever, unrelated motions (for good cause), and injunctions against imminent public-health or public-safety threats are not blocked by the stay.","c":"KRS 454.466","v":true},{"t":"Hearing within 60 days; ruling within 60 days after the hearing","d":"The court must hear the motion within 60 days after filing, unless it orders a later hearing to allow limited discovery (then within 60 days of that order) or for good cause. It must rule within 60 days after the hearing. The court considers the pleadings, the motion, any response or reply, and any evidence admissible on summary judgment.","c":"KRS 454.468, 454.470, 454.474","v":true},{"t":"Three-step test; dismissal is with prejudice","d":"The court must dismiss with prejudice if three things are true. First, the movant shows the statute applies under KRS 454.462(1). Second, the respondent fails to show an exclusion applies under KRS 454.462(2). Third, either the respondent fails to establish a prima facie case on each essential element, or the movant shows a failure to state a claim or no genuine issue of material fact. A voluntary dismissal without prejudice does not defeat the motion or the fee claim. A voluntary dismissal with prejudice makes the movant the prevailing party for fees.","c":"KRS 454.472","v":true},{"t":"Immediate appeal as of right by either side","d":"Any party may appeal as of right from an order granting or denying the motion in whole or in part. The statute says the appeal is filed ‘in accordance with the Kentucky Rules of Civil Procedure’. Since January 1, 2023, appellate procedure has been in the Rules of Appellate Procedure (RAP 1(A); CR 1(2)), so expect a RAP 3 notice of appeal within 30 days. No collected text addresses that wording mismatch.","c":"KRS 454.476; RAP 3(A)(1)","v":true},{"t":"Fees: mandatory for a winning movant; for a respondent only if the motion lacked good cause","d":"On the motion or an appeal of the order, the court shall award court costs, reasonable attorney’s fees, and reasonable litigation expenses related to the motion. A prevailing movant always gets them. A prevailing respondent gets them only if the court finds the motion was brought without good cause.","c":"KRS 454.478","v":true}],"oos":[{"t":"Pro hac vice: KBA fee certification first, then a motion to the trial court","d":"An out-of-state lawyer may practice a case in Kentucky only by doing three things. The lawyer must submit to the jurisdiction and rules of the Supreme Court of Kentucky. The lawyer must pay the Kentucky Bar Association a per-case fee equal to the annual dues of KBA members admitted five years or more. And the lawyer must engage a KBA member as co-counsel, who must be present at all trials and whenever the court requires.\nNo court may grant the motion for permission to practice until the lawyer submits a KBA certification that the fee was received. As posted on November 10, 2026, the fee is $310 per case per attorney plus a $9.30 processing fee, paid online through a KBA pro hac vice account.\nThe rule text (SCR 3.030(2)) was not collected. This summary relies on the KBA’s restatement of it.","c":"SCR 3.030(2) (as restated by the KBA Pro Hac Vice Certification page)","v":true},{"t":"Annual renewal fee, with automatic suspension 30 days after it is missed","d":"If the case is still going, the pro hac vice lawyer must pay a renewal fee on each anniversary of the original admission, equal to the same 5-year dues amount. If the fee is not paid within 30 days of the due date, the lawyer is suspended from appearing in every case in which the lawyer is admitted pro hac vice. Kentucky co-counsel must notify the court immediately upon learning of the non-payment.","c":"SCR 3.030(3)(a)–(b) (AO 2022-11)","v":true},{"t":"KBA mechanics: permanent PHV ID, receipt to the court, close-out notice","d":"Since January 1, 2016, each pro hac vice lawyer has a permanent Pro Hac Vice ID number, which must be given on every later certification request. The KBA emails a letter or receipt certifying payment. Both the lawyer and Kentucky co-counsel are responsible for getting it to the court. When the appearance ends, the lawyer updates the case on the KBA dashboard.","c":"KBA Pro Hac Vice Certification page (Nov. 10, 2026)","v":true},{"t":"eFiling registration does not list pro hac vice lawyers","d":"Registration with the KY eCourts eFiling system is limited to four groups: licensed attorneys in good standing with the KBA, non-attorneys delegated access by a registered attorney, self-represented parties, and trusted submitters (court personnel). Out-of-state lawyers admitted pro hac vice are not on the list, so plan for Kentucky co-counsel to eFile. Registering creates consent to electronic service of eFilings (§ 6(1)(g)). In an existing case, however, consent begins only after you eFile an entry of appearance or other eligible document (§ 13(1)).","c":"eFiling Rules §§ 6(1)(a), 6(1)(g), 13(1) (AO 2025-02)","v":true},{"t":"Kentucky co-counsel must put a 5-digit KBA member number on every filing","d":"Every Kentucky-licensed attorney must include the 5-digit member identification number on all filings with Kentucky courts. Members must also keep an official Bar Roster address that allows physical service and an official email address, and update each within 10 days of a change.","c":"SCR 3.035(1)(a)–(c) (AO 2022-43)","v":true},{"t":"In-house counsel: limited certificate (SCR 2.111)","d":"A lawyer admitted elsewhere who works in Kentucky solely for an employer and its affiliates must apply for a limited certificate. The fee is $1,500 plus KBA dues. The lawyer becomes an active KBA member but may serve only the employer and approved pro bono programs (without fee). To appear as attorney of record for the employer in a Kentucky court, the lawyer must engage fully admitted Kentucky co-counsel. The certificate survives a job change only if the lawyer files notice of the new employer within 30 days.","c":"SCR 2.111(1)–(7) (AO 2025-06)","v":true},{"t":"Admission without examination: 5 of the last 7 years, reciprocity required","d":"A lawyer who has actively practiced 5 of the 7 years before applying, in a jurisdiction with reciprocity or comity with Kentucky, may be admitted without examination. The fee is $1,500, non-refundable, and the applicant must pass character and fitness review. The applicant’s main practice jurisdiction must have reciprocal-admission rules no more restrictive than Kentucky’s. Full-time teaching at an ABA-accredited law school counts as practice.","c":"SCR 2.110 (AO 2025-06)","v":true},{"t":"Practice pending admission: up to one year, with mandatory disclaimers","d":"A lawyer in good standing elsewhere who has applied under SCR 2.090 (UBE transfer) or 2.110 may practice for up to one year while the application is pending. Conditions include a continuous Kentucky presence, association with a Kentucky lawyer, notice to the KBA within 30 days of setting up an office, and paying the fee. All written communications must carry the line ‘Practice temporarily authorized pending admission to the Kentucky Bar Association’.","c":"SCR 2.117 (AO 2025-06)","v":true},{"t":"Public-sector lawyers: 18-month limited admission (amended Oct. 14, 2026)","d":"A lawyer admitted elsewhere who joins an organized public defender program, a legal services program for the indigent, the Attorney General, or a Commonwealth’s or County Attorney may be specially admitted for that office’s matters only. The fee is $100. Admission runs in 18-month terms, renewable until the lawyer is eligible for admission without examination under SCR 2.110. It ends at once if the employment ends.","c":"SCR 2.112 (AO 2026-36)","v":true},{"t":"Mandatory eFiling in probate and estate case types; others by separate order","d":"Supported case types may be eFiled, and some are made mandatory by separate Supreme Court order. Since January 1, 2024, attorneys must eFile all eligible documents in listed probate/estate case types: guardianship, conservatorship and trusteeship, testate and intestate probate, dispensing with administration, name change, and will contests. From February 1, 2024, circuit clerks reject conventionally filed eligible documents in those case types. The exception is a filing that meets a jurisdictional deadline and comes with AOC Form 108 (Affidavit of Necessity). Earlier mandatory-eFiling orders (AO 2022-22, 2022-45, 2022-51, 2023-04) were not collected. Check them for other case types.","c":"eFiling Rules § 3(3) (AO 2025-02); AO 2023-39","v":true},{"t":"Limited-scope representation: not verified","d":"Kentucky’s versions of Rule 1.2(c) and any civil rule on limited appearances (CR 11 or similar) were not in the collected texts, because the consolidated SCR 3.130 and CR are published officially only on Westlaw, which was excluded. Check SCR 3.130(1.2) and CR 11 before relying on a limited-scope engagement.","c":"SCR 3.130(1.2) (not collected)","v":false}],"ethics":[{"t":"Governing code: SCR 3.130, the Kentucky Rules of Professional Conduct","d":"Kentucky’s ethics rules are Supreme Court Rule 3.130, cited by rule number in parentheses, for example SCR 3.130(1.1), and each rule carries ‘Supreme Court Commentary’. The amendment orders collected follow the ABA Model Rules numbering through Part 8, but the consolidated text was not collected. This summary therefore lists only differences shown in amendment orders and does not claim to be complete.","c":"SCR 3.130 (AO 2022-11 § X; AO 2022-43; AO 2025-04)","v":false},{"t":"Rule 5.7 is ‘Activities of Suspended Lawyer’, and law-related services moved to 5.8","d":"Unlike the ABA Model Rules, where 5.7 covers law-related services, Kentucky’s Rule 5.7 regulates work by suspended lawyers. A lawyer may not employ, associate professionally with, or aid a suspended lawyer in doing prohibited acts. A lawyer also may not employ or associate with a suspended member who was with the lawyer or firm when suspended. The commentary lists permitted preparatory and clerical tasks. Kentucky’s law-related-services rule, matching ABA 5.7, was added as SCR 3.130(5.8) in 2022.","c":"SCR 3.130(5.7)(b) & cmt. 4; SCR 3.130(5.8) (AO 2022-11)","v":true},{"t":"Prosecutors must report charged lawyers to Bar Counsel","d":"A lawyer prosecuting any KBA member who has been arrested for, or charged by indictment, information, or complaint with, a felony or Class A misdemeanor must immediately notify Bar Counsel. The ABA Model Rule 8.3 has no counterpart.","c":"SCR 3.130(8.3)(f) (AO 2022-43)","v":true},{"t":"Sale of a practice: seller must arrange for trust-account records","d":"On sale of a law practice, the seller must make reasonable arrangements for maintaining client trust account records. The separate Kentucky Rule 1.20 on the same subject was deleted and folded into this new paragraph (g).","c":"SCR 3.130(1.17)(g); former SCR 3.130(1.20) (AO 2022-43)","v":true},{"t":"Malpractice insurance disclosure every year by September 1","d":"Every active member must certify by September 1 whether they are in private practice and, if so, whether they carry professional liability insurance of at least $100,000 per claim and $300,000 aggregate. The disclosure is made public. Missing the October 1 certification brings a $50 late fee. A lapse in reported coverage must be reported within 30 days. Lawyers working only for a government entity or an organization client are exempt.","c":"SCR 3.023 (AO 2022-54)","v":true},{"t":"Competence commentary on lawyer well-being (2025)","d":"New commentary [11] to Rule 1.1 says that a lawyer’s mental, emotional, and physical well-being is an important aspect of competence. It points to the Kentucky Lawyer Assistance Program and to Rules 1.16(a)(2), 5.1, 5.2, 5.3 and 5.8.","c":"SCR 3.130(1.1) cmt. [11] (AO 2025-04)","v":true},{"t":"Trial publicity: Kentucky commentary lists presumptively prejudicial subjects","d":"Rule 3.6(a) is phrased like the ABA rule: it bars extrajudicial statements the lawyer knows or should know will be publicly disseminated and have a substantial likelihood of materially prejudicing the proceeding. Kentucky commentary 5 lists subjects that are more likely than not to be prejudicial. These include a party’s or witness’s character or criminal record, test results, opinions on guilt, likely-inadmissible information, and stating that a defendant is charged without adding that the charge is only an accusation.","c":"SCR 3.130(3.6)(a) & cmt. 5 (AO 2022-11)","v":true},{"t":"Advertising is overseen by an Attorneys’ Advertising Commission","d":"Kentucky’s Part 7 includes SCR 3.130(7.02), titled ‘Attorneys’ advertising commission’. The commission’s filing and fee requirements were not in the collected texts. Check the KBA advertising rules page and SCR 3.130(7.01)–(7.50) before advertising in Kentucky.","c":"SCR 3.130(7.02) (title only, AO 2022-43)","v":false}],"style":"Commonwealth of Kentucky","home":"https://www.kycourts.gov/","unique":{"mines":[{"t":"The appeal clock runs from the clerk’s docket notation of service, not entry","d":"The 30 days to appeal run from the date of the clerk’s docket notation of service of the judgment under CR 77.04(2). A timely notice of appeal is jurisdictional, and a late one is dismissed. Failing to receive notice does not extend time, except that the trial court may add up to 10 days for excusable neglect in learning of entry.","c":"RAP 2(A)(2)–(3), 3(A)(1)–(2), 3(C)–(D); CR 77.04(2), (4)"},{"t":"Only CR 50.02, 52.02 and 59 motions toll; CR 60.02 does not","d":"Time to appeal restarts for all parties only from the order disposing of the last timely CR 50.02, CR 52.02 or CR 59 motion, and not when a new trial is granted. No motion under any other civil rule tolls. To challenge the post-judgment order, you must file a new or amended notice of appeal.","c":"RAP 3(E)(2), (E)(4)"},{"t":"Cross-appeal: 10 days after the appeal deadline, and no extensions","d":"A notice of cross-appeal is due no later than 10 days after the last day allowed for the notice of appeal. It does not run from service of the opponent’s notice. Courts may not extend or enlarge time under RAP 3, 4 or 17, even for excusable neglect. Without a cross-appeal, an appellee cannot seek to enlarge its own rights.","c":"RAP 4(B)–(C), 6(C)–(D)"},{"t":"Notice of appeal is not docketed until the fee is paid","d":"Pay the fee to the clerk of the court being appealed from when you tender the notice. The notice is not docketed or noted as filed until payment, unless an in forma pauperis motion accompanies it. The fee is $150 for appeals to the Court of Appeals or Supreme Court and $60 for district court appeals, as in the 2022 text. Check for later fee orders.","c":"RAP 2(I) (AO 2026-01), 3(A)(3), 13(B), 48(B)(4)"},{"t":"Temporary injunction rulings: 20 days to move the Court of Appeals","d":"There is no appellate review of a CR 65.03 restraining order. A party hurt by an order granting, denying, modifying or dissolving a temporary injunction must move the Court of Appeals within 20 days of entry. Further review in the Supreme Court is due within 10 days and requires extraordinary cause. Rulings under RAP 20 are not reconsidered. The old CR 65.07–65.09 procedures were deleted.","c":"RAP 20(A), (B), (E), (F)(1); AO 2022-49 § II"},{"t":"Anti-SLAPP: 60 days from service, automatic stay, and mandatory fees","d":"A defendant who waits past 60 days after service needs good cause to file the special motion. Plaintiffs should note that filing the motion stays discovery automatically, and a losing plaintiff pays the movant’s fees and expenses.","c":"KRS 454.464, 454.466(1), 454.478(1)"},{"t":"Pro hac vice lapses if the annual renewal goes unpaid","d":"The per-case fee recurs on each anniversary of admission while the case continues. Thirty days after a missed payment, the lawyer is suspended from appearing in every Kentucky pro hac vice case. Kentucky co-counsel must tell the court.","c":"SCR 3.030(3)(a)–(b)"},{"t":"eFiling deadline is midnight in the receiving court’s time zone; e-service adds 3 days","d":"An eFiling is filed when the system’s Notice of Electronic Filing (NEF) says it is. It must be completed before midnight in the time zone of the receiving court, and a judge may set an earlier time. Kentucky courts sit in both the Eastern and Central time zones. Emailing a document to the clerk or judge is not filing. Electronic service is treated like mail service under CR 6.05, which adds 3 days. Fix a document the clerk returns within 2 business days to keep the original filing time.","c":"eFiling Rules §§ 8(2)(a)–(c), 8(3)(b), 13(7) (AO 2025-02)"},{"t":"Appellate briefs: paper-style formatting, colored covers, word-count certificate","d":"Briefs need a 1½-inch left margin and 12-point text and footnotes, and must be bound at the left. Covers are color-coded: red for appellant, blue for appellee, yellow for reply. In the Court of Appeals, initial and response briefs are limited to 8,750 words or 20 computer-generated pages, and replies to 1,750 words or 4 pages. A computer-generated brief over the page limit needs a RAP 15 word-count certificate. Briefs must start the argument with a statement showing how each issue was preserved, with record cites.","c":"RAP 15(C), 31(A), (C)(2), (G)(2) (AO 2026-01), 32(A)(4)"},{"t":"If the appellee does not file a brief, the court may treat it as a confession of error","d":"If the appellee’s brief is late, the court may accept the appellant’s statement of facts and issues, may reverse if the appellant’s brief reasonably appears to support reversal, or may treat the failure as a confession of error and reverse without reaching the merits.","c":"RAP 31(H)(3)"},{"t":"Citing unpublished Kentucky opinions takes four conditions and the case number","d":"You may cite a ‘Not To Be Published’ Kentucky opinion only if all four conditions hold: it was rendered after January 1, 2003; it is final; no published opinion adequately addresses the point; and you say it is not binding. Give the style, date and case number. Unpublished out-of-state opinions need a URL or a full copy in the appendix.","c":"RAP 41(A), (C)"},{"t":"Court of Appeals prehearing conference: 20-day motion window that changes the briefing clock","d":"Since April 1, 2026, a prehearing conference in civil appeals is by optional motion with a prehearing statement, filed within 20 days of the notice of appeal or cross-appeal. Some case types are excluded. Further steps do not run until the Court of Appeals orders. If no motion is filed, the time runs from the clerk’s notice saying so. Record designation and certification deadlines are keyed to the end of the prehearing procedure, and RAP 24 and 26 still cite the pre-2026 numbering, so confirm dates with the clerk.","c":"RAP 22(A)–(B) (AO 2026-01); RAP 24(A)(1)(b), 26(B)(4)"}],"judge":[{"t":"No peremptory strike; statutory disqualification plus an affidavit route to the Chief Justice","d":"No collected text gives a peremptory change of judge. Under KRS 26A.015(2), a judge must step aside for personal bias, personal knowledge of disputed facts, or having expressed an opinion on the merits. Other grounds include prior work as a lawyer in the matter, a financial interest held by the judge, spouse, or minor child, close relatives involved as parties, lawyers, or likely material witnesses, and any circumstance in which impartiality might reasonably be questioned. The Chief Justice replaces a disqualified judge.\nSeparately, under KRS 26A.020(1) a party may file with the circuit clerk an affidavit that the judge will not afford a fair and impartial trial. The clerk certifies the facts to the Chief Justice, who reviews them and decides whether to designate a special judge, so this is not automatic. The statute sets no filing deadline. Timeliness rules from case law were not collected.","c":"KRS 26A.015(2)–(3); KRS 26A.020(1)","v":true},{"t":"Appellate settlement judge cannot sit on the panel","d":"A Court of Appeals judge who takes part in a RAP 22 prehearing conference or settlement discussions may not sit on the panel that hears the appeal.","c":"RAP 22(D)(5) (as amended by AO 2026-01)","v":true}],"speech":[{"t":"Kentucky constitutional speech protections: not established here","d":"No collected text addresses whether the Kentucky Constitution’s free-speech provisions are read more broadly than the First Amendment. The anti-SLAPP statute expressly covers rights ‘guaranteed by the United States Constitution or Kentucky Constitution’ (KRS 454.462(1)(c)), but that does not define their scope.","c":"KRS 454.462(1)(c); Ky. Const. (not collected)","v":false}],"other":[{"t":"Mediation rules are part of the Civil Rules (CR 99–100)","d":"Since February 1, 2022, court-ordered mediation is governed by CR 99, and mediator conduct by CR 100. Courts may not refer every case, or every case of a type, to mediation, and may not make mediation a condition of getting a trial date. Referral does not stay discovery. Parties have 15 days to agree on a mediator. CR 99.08(4) says an insured party must have a carrier representative present with full authority, who is not outside counsel. The next paragraph, however, says that unless the court expressly orders it, a represented party and the carrier representative need not be present. Read the court’s order closely. A mediation may be entirely virtual unless the parties agree or the court orders otherwise. Mediation communications are privileged, and the mediator’s privilege cannot be waived by the parties.","c":"CR 99.03, 99.04, 99.05, 99.08, 99.11; CR 100 (AO 2022-04, 2022-08, 2022-58)","v":true}]},"appeals":{"asof":"November 10, 2026","src":"Kentucky Rules of Appellate Procedure (RAP), adopted by Ky. S. Ct. Admin. Order 2022-49 (effective January 1, 2023), as amended by Admin. Order 2026-01 (effective April 1, 2026). Also CR 77.04 as rewritten by AO 2022-49; the Administrative Rules of Practice and Procedure for the Kentucky Court of Justice Electronic Filing (AO 2025-02, Section 15 replaced by AO 2025-41); and KRS 454.476. Texts are the official PDFs on kycourts.gov and legislature.ky.gov. No other RAP amendment order appears in the Supreme Court’s administrative-order index through AO 2026-39. Filing fees are as stated in RAP 13 in 2022; no later appellate-fee order was collected.","intro":{"t":"Appeals in Kentucky: a 30-day jurisdictional notice, counted from the clerk’s docket notation","d":"Civil appeals from circuit court go to the Court of Appeals as of right. Civil cases reach the Supreme Court mainly by motion for discretionary review (RAP 44) or by a motion to transfer filed within 10 days of the notice of appeal (RAP 17). District court judgments are appealed to circuit court (RAP 48). Since January 1, 2023, all appellate procedure has been in the RAP, and the old CR 72–76 were deleted.\nOnly the timely filing of a notice of appeal, cross-appeal, or motion for discretionary review is jurisdictional. Any other rule violation does not void the appeal but can bring sanctions, from a deficiency notice up to fines of $1,000 or dismissal (RAP 2(A)(2), RAP 10). Briefs are still built to paper specifications, with colored covers, bound copies, and a 1½-inch left margin, even when eFiled."},"groups":[["Which court and how the appeal is taken",[{"t":"Notice of appeal in the court below","d":"File the notice of appeal in the court from which the appeal is taken. Civil appeals go to the next higher court, and family court appeals go to the Court of Appeals. The notice must name the appellants, identify and date the judgment or order appealed, and certify service on all parties. It should also attach the judgment, name the appellate court and the other parties and counsel, and attach any orders on tolling motions under CR 50.02, 52.02 or 59. Joint appellants must all sign.","c":"RAP 2(A)(1), (B), (F); RAP 5(C)"},{"t":"Who becomes a party","d":"A timely notice from a final order on all claims brings in every party below, except those dismissed by an earlier final order. A notice from a judgment made final on fewer than all claims or parties under CR 54.02(1) brings in only the parties against whom that judgment runs.","c":"RAP 2(A)(2)"},{"t":"Appealing does not waive personal-jurisdiction or service defenses","d":"If the trial court denied a CR 12.02 defense of lack of personal jurisdiction, improper venue, insufficient process, or insufficient service, taking an appeal is not an entry of appearance in any court.","c":"RAP 2(E)"},{"t":"Transfer to the Supreme Court (bypass)","d":"Within 10 days after a notice of appeal to the Court of Appeals is filed, any party may move the Supreme Court to transfer the case, attaching the notice of appeal. Transfer is granted only for great and immediate public importance. The motion suspends further steps until the order on it. The fee is $150, and the 10 days cannot be extended.","c":"RAP 17(A), (C)–(D), (H); RAP 6(C)–(D); RAP 13(B)(1)(c)"},{"t":"Appeals from district court","d":"Appeals from district court go to circuit court. File the notice in district court within the RAP 3 time or a shorter statutory time, such as small claims (KRS 24A.340) 10 days, paternity (KRS 406.051) 60 days, or forcible detainer (KRS 383.255) 7 days. The fee is $60 plus other fees. Within 30 days after the notice, the appellant must file a statement of appeal (8,750 words or 20 pages). Appellees may file a counterstatement within 30 days after that. Further review is by motion for discretionary review in the Court of Appeals within 30 days after the circuit court judgment.","c":"RAP 48(B), (F); RAP 44(B)(1)"},{"t":"Joint, heard-together, and consolidated appeals (rewritten 2026)","d":"Appeals ‘heard together’ by the same panel keep separate briefs. ‘Consolidated’ appeals are joined for all purposes: all appellants file one joint brief and one joint reply, and each appellee files one response. Consolidation is generally granted only for appeals from the same circuit court action with identical parties.","c":"RAP 2(F)–(H) (AO 2026-01)"}]],["Notice of appeal: time and trigger",[{"t":"30 days from the docket notation of service","d":"Unless a statute or rule says otherwise, the notice of appeal is due no later than 30 days from the date of the clerk’s notation of service of the judgment or order. Under CR 77.04(2), that notation date counts as the date of entry for appeal purposes, or the date a waiver of notice was filed if earlier. The clerk must serve notice of entry of every judgment, final order, and order that affects appeal time.","c":"RAP 3(A)(1)–(2); CR 77.04(1)–(2)"},{"t":"Not getting notice does not help, except for a 10-day extension","d":"Failure to serve or receive notice of entry does not affect appeal time. On a showing of excusable neglect based on failing to learn of entry, the trial court may extend the time by up to 10 days past the original deadline. CR 60.02 and other remedies remain available.","c":"RAP 3(C)–(D); CR 77.04(4)"},{"t":"Filing fee at tender; in forma pauperis","d":"Pay the fee to the trial clerk when you tender the notice, or it will not be docketed. A notice accompanied by a timely in forma pauperis motion is treated as timely. If the motion is denied, the party has 30 days to pay or to appeal the denial.","c":"RAP 2(I) (AO 2026-01); RAP 3(A)(3); RAP 54–55"},{"t":"Appellate filing fees (2022 text)","d":"The fee is $150 each for an appeal, cross-appeal, certification of law, transfer motion, motion for discretionary review or cross-motion, rehearing petition, amicus motion, RAP 20 motion, original action, or motion to reconsider a final order. Some family-division appeals (paternity, dependency/neglect/abuse, domestic violence, status offense) are $75, and district court appeals are $60. No fee is charged for a motion to publish.","c":"RAP 13(B); RAP 43(H)"}]],["Post-judgment motions that stop the clock",[{"t":"Only CR 50.02, 52.02 and 59 toll","d":"If any party timely files a motion under CR 50.02, CR 52.02 or CR 59, appeal time runs for everyone from entry of the order disposing of the last such motion. A CR 59 order granting a new trial does not restart it. No motion under any other civil rule tolls the time.","c":"RAP 3(E)(2)"},{"t":"Premature notice: ask for abeyance","d":"If you file the notice of appeal while a tolling motion is pending, the trial court keeps jurisdiction to decide it. The appellant must promptly move the appellate court to hold the appeal in abeyance, then file the trial court’s order when entered. To challenge that order or an amended judgment, file a new or amended notice within 30 days of the notation of service of the disposing order. No added fee is charged for an amended notice.","c":"RAP 3(E)(3)–(5)"}]],["Cross-appeals",[{"t":"10 days after the last day for the notice of appeal","d":"Any party to the appeal may cross-appeal. The notice is due no later than 10 days from the last day allowed for the notice of appeal. A party first affected by someone else’s cross-appeal has 10 days from that notice to file an additional cross-appeal. Without a cross-appeal, a party cannot seek to enlarge its own rights or reduce an adversary’s. A cross-appeal survives dismissal of the main appeal.","c":"RAP 4(A)–(E); RAP 7(G)(2)(c)"},{"t":"No extensions","d":"Courts may not extend or enlarge time for actions under RAP 3, 4 or 17, even for excusable neglect. Enlargement is also barred for RAP 44 motions for discretionary review.","c":"RAP 6(C)–(D)"}]],["Prehearing conference and the record",[{"t":"Prehearing conference by optional motion (since April 1, 2026)","d":"In most civil appeals to the Court of Appeals, any party may move for a prehearing conference with a prehearing statement (court form) within 20 days of the notice of appeal or cross-appeal. Other parties may respond within 10 days. Excluded are criminal cases, CR 23.06 class-certification appeals, prisoner, election, contempt, and writ appeals, family-type orders, and cases transferred to the Supreme Court.\nUntil the Court of Appeals orders otherwise, further steps do not run, except a cross-appeal, a transfer motion, or the prehearing motion itself. If no motion is filed, time runs from the clerk’s notice saying so. Conference discussions are confidential.","c":"RAP 22(A)–(D) (AO 2026-01)"},{"t":"Designating recordings and certifying the record","d":"The appellant must designate the dates of pre- and post-trial recordings needed. The deadline is within 10 days of the notice of appeal, or, where RAP 22 applies, within 10 days of the order ending prehearing procedure. Other parties may counter-designate within 10 days. Trial recordings are included automatically. The clerk certifies the record within 30 days after the first notice of appeal, or 30 days after prehearing procedure ends. RAP 24 and 26 still cite ‘RAP 22(B)(2)’, which the 2026 rewrite renumbered.","c":"RAP 24(A)(1)–(2); RAP 26(B)(4)–(5)"},{"t":"Video record","d":"Where proceedings are video-recorded, two recordings form the official record, and duplicates are available for counsel for a fee. Briefs cite recordings as ‘VR mm/dd/yy at hh:mm:ss’ and the written record as ‘TR’ pages.","c":"CR 98(2) (AO 2022-49); RAP 31(E)(3)–(4)"}]],["Briefing schedule",[{"t":"Ordinary civil appeal","d":"The appellant’s brief is due 60 days after the docket notation of the clerk’s notice of certification of the record. In the Supreme Court after discretionary review is granted, it is due 60 days from the order granting review. The appellee’s brief is due 60 days after the appellant’s brief. The reply is due 15 days after the last appellee brief, or after it was due where the reply answers several appellees.","c":"RAP 30(C)(1) (reply as amended by AO 2026-01)"},{"t":"With a cross-appeal","d":"The appellee/cross-appellant’s combined brief is due 60 days after the appellant’s brief. The appellant’s combined reply/cross-appellee brief is due 60 days after the last appellee brief was filed or due. The cross-appellant’s reply is due 15 days after that.","c":"RAP 30(C)(2)"},{"t":"Expedited appeals: 30/30/10","d":"Appeals from orders on adoption, termination of parental rights, paternity, dependency/abuse/neglect, domestic violence, juvenile status offenses, and CR 23.06 class-certification orders are expedited. The appellant has 30 days after the notice of certification, the appellee 30 days, and the reply 10 days, with combined briefs on the same 30-day pattern. Extensions are considered only in extraordinary circumstances.","c":"RAP 30(E)"},{"t":"Service of briefs, including on the trial judge","d":"Serve each brief on every party and on the judge whose decision is under review. Electronic service is allowed on the trial judge and on consenting parties.","c":"RAP 30(B)"},{"t":"Amicus curiae","d":"An amicus brief needs leave. The motion and brief are due within 15 days after the appellant’s brief is filed or due, whichever is later, and the motion carries the $150 fee. The brief has a tan cover and a 5,250-word limit with a certificate, and no appendix except unpublished opinions. Amicus counsel may not argue.","c":"RAP 34"}]],["Length, format and content",[{"t":"Court of Appeals limits","d":"Initial and response briefs: 8,750 words or 20 pages if computer-generated (25 pages if handwritten or typed). Reply: 1,750 words or 4 pages, plus 1,750 words or 4 pages for each additional appellee brief answered. Appellee response combined with cross-appeal: 14,000 words or 30 pages. Appellant reply combined with cross-appellee response: 10,500 words or 25 pages.","c":"RAP 31(G)(2) (AO 2026-01)"},{"t":"Supreme Court limits","d":"Initial and response briefs: 17,500 words or 40 pages. Reply: 3,500 words or 7 pages, plus 1,750 words or 4 pages for each additional appellee brief. Appellee response combined with cross-appeal: 22,750 words or 50 pages. Appellant reply combined with cross-appellee response: 19,250 words or 45 pages, raised in 2026 from 8,750 words or 20 pages. Death-penalty briefs may be extended to 52,500 words on a motion filed at least 20 days before the due date.","c":"RAP 31(G)(3)–(4) (AO 2026-01)"},{"t":"Word-count certificate and exclusions","d":"A computer-generated brief within the page limit needs no certificate. A brief over the page limit but within the word limit needs a certificate stating the word count, in the RAP 15(E) form. The cover, introduction, oral-argument statement, statement of points and authorities, signature block, exhibits, and appendices do not count.","c":"RAP 15(B)–(E); RAP 31(G)(1), (5)"},{"t":"Physical format and covers","d":"Use 8½ × 11 unglazed white paper, single-sided and double-spaced, with 12-point text and footnotes, a 1½-inch left margin and 1-inch other margins, bound at the left. Covers are red for appellant, blue for appellee and combined appellee/cross-appellant, and yellow for replies. The front cover carries the certificate of service and, in most courts, a certificate that the record was returned. File 1 unbound and 4 bound copies in the Court of Appeals, and 1 unbound and 9 bound copies in the Supreme Court, plus 1 for each cross-appeal.","c":"RAP 31(A), (C), (F)"},{"t":"Required parts of the appellant’s brief","d":"The appellant’s brief has these parts, in order: an introduction and oral-argument statement (one page combined); a statement of points and authorities (required if the brief exceeds 1,750 words since 2026, previously 5 pages); a statement of the case with record cites; argument opening each issue with a statement of whether and how it was preserved; conclusion; signature; and an appendix. The appendix is tabbed and indexed, and the judgment under review comes first after the index.","c":"RAP 32(A), (D) (AO 2026-01), (E)"},{"t":"Name redaction","d":"CR 7.03 applies. Use initials or a descriptive term instead of names in cases involving juveniles, abuse and neglect, termination of parental rights, mental health, and appeals from judgments granting expungement.","c":"RAP 5(B)(2), 7(B), 31(B) (AO 2026-01)"},{"t":"Consequences of noncompliance","d":"A brief that does not substantially comply may be stricken. A missing appellant’s brief can mean dismissal. A missing appellee’s brief lets the court accept the appellant’s facts, reverse if the appellant’s brief reasonably supports it, or treat the failure as a confession of error.","c":"RAP 31(H); RAP 10(B)"}]],["Motions, time computation, extensions",[{"t":"Motions practice","d":"Motions must be in writing with particular grounds. Responses are due within 10 days of filing, and the court may hear oral argument on a motion only by order. File 1 unbound and 4 bound copies in either court unless RAP 43–46 say otherwise. A motion to dismiss an appeal suspends the running of time for other steps until it is denied or passed to the merits. A criminal appellant’s voluntary dismissal needs a sworn affidavit from the client.","c":"RAP 7 (AO 2026-01)"},{"t":"Counting days and asking for more time","d":"Do not count the trigger day. A last day falling on a weekend or legal holiday rolls forward. For periods under 7 days, skip intermediate weekends and holidays. Parties cannot extend time by agreement. Before a deadline, the court may extend for cause. After it, the court may enlarge only for excusable neglect. Neither is available for RAP 3, 4 or 17, and enlargement is not available for RAP 44.","c":"RAP 6"},{"t":"Deficiency notices: 10 days to fix","d":"If the Supreme Court or Court of Appeals issues a deficiency notice, file the corrected document within 10 days of the notice.","c":"RAP 5(F)"},{"t":"Mailbox rule for appellate clerks only","d":"A document filed with the Supreme Court or Court of Appeals clerk is timely if sent by U.S. registered mail (not certified) or express mail, or by another recognized carrier, and the carrier’s receipt date on the outside of the container falls within the filing period. Notices of appeal are filed in the trial court and must actually be received there in time.","c":"RAP 5(C), (E)"}]],["Interlocutory review",[{"t":"Temporary injunctions: RAP 20 motion within 20 days","d":"There is no appellate review of a CR 65.03 restraining order. A party adversely affected by an interlocutory order granting, denying, modifying or dissolving a temporary injunction may move the Court of Appeals within 20 days of entry. The trial court may suspend a dissolution order for up to 20 days to allow this. Responses are due in 10 days, and the motion is served on the trial judge. Emergency relief is available on a separate motion. RAP 20 rulings are not reconsidered. Supreme Court review is discretionary, must be sought within 10 days, and requires extraordinary cause.","c":"RAP 20(A)–(F)"},{"t":"Permanent injunctions pending appeal","d":"After appealing a final judgment granting or denying an injunction, first ask the circuit court to grant, suspend, or modify relief pending appeal. Then the adversely affected party may move the Court of Appeals.","c":"RAP 20(C)"},{"t":"Intermediate relief while an appeal is pending","d":"After a notice of appeal or a motion for discretionary review, a party facing immediate and irreparable injury may move for intermediate relief, attaching the judgment and any stay papers from below. Responses are due in 10 days, and no replies are allowed. Review of a Court of Appeals ruling follows RAP 20(F).","c":"RAP 21"},{"t":"Other immediately appealable orders seen in the collected texts","d":"These include a judgment made final on fewer than all claims or parties under CR 54.02(1) (RAP 2(A)(2)); orders granting or denying class certification under CR 23.06, which are expedited (RAP 30(E)); and orders granting or denying an anti-SLAPP special motion, appealable as of right by any party (KRS 454.476). The full text of CR 54.02 and CR 23.06 was not collected.","c":"RAP 2(A)(2); RAP 30(E); KRS 454.476"},{"t":"Writs (original actions)","d":"Original actions lie in an appellate court only against a judge or agency whose decisions that court reviews as of right. The petition is limited to 14,000 words or 30 pages, the response is due in 20 days, and no reply is allowed. A final Court of Appeals order in an original action may be appealed to the Supreme Court within 30 days, briefed on the expedited schedule.","c":"RAP 60(A)–(G), (I)"}]],["Rehearing, finality and opinions",[{"t":"Petition for rehearing, modification or extension: 20 days","d":"A petition for rehearing, modification, extension, or publication is due within 20 days of the opinion, and a response within 20 days of the petition. Rehearing is limited to a material fact, controlling statute, or decision the court overlooked, or a misconception of the issues. The limit is 3,500 words or 8 pages, with a green cover (gray for the response), and the fee is $150. Orders, as opposed to opinions, are challenged by a motion for reconsideration within 20 days.","c":"RAP 43(B)(1)–(5), (D), (F)"},{"t":"When opinions become final","d":"A Supreme Court opinion is final on the 21st day after rendition. A Court of Appeals opinion is final on the 31st day, unless a timely rehearing petition or motion for discretionary review is filed. A timely motion for discretionary review suspends finality. No mandate issues: the clerk sends the final opinion to the trial court.","c":"RAP 40(G)(1)–(6)"},{"t":"Citing unpublished opinions","d":"‘Not To Be Published’ Kentucky opinions are not binding and are disfavored. You may cite one only if it was rendered after January 1, 2003, is final, addresses a point no published opinion adequately covers, and you state it is not binding. Give the style, date and case number. A motion to publish is due within 20 days of the opinion.","c":"RAP 40(D), 41, 43(H)"}]],["Review in the Supreme Court",[{"t":"Motion for discretionary review: 30 days","d":"File within 30 days after the Court of Appeals decision. If a timely rehearing or reconsideration filing is pending, file within 30 days after its denial or after the final decision on rehearing. The limit is 5,250 words or 13 pages, with 10 copies, and the fee is $150. The response is due in 30 days, and no reply is allowed. Review is granted only for special reasons. Rulings on these motions are not reconsidered, and the clerk will not accept a reconsideration motion. While a motion for discretionary review is pending, a party cannot also seek rehearing in the Court of Appeals.","c":"RAP 44(A)–(K); RAP 43(G)"},{"t":"Cross-motion for discretionary review: 10 days after a grant","d":"If review is granted, the respondent has 10 days to file a cross-motion designating additional issues. This suspends the briefing time until the order on the cross-motion.","c":"RAP 46(A), (D)"},{"t":"Briefing after a grant","d":"The movant becomes the appellant. Briefs run from the order granting review, starting with 60 days for the appellant.","c":"RAP 44(J)(1); RAP 30(C)(1)(a)"},{"t":"Certified questions","d":"Federal courts and the highest courts of other states or D.C. may ask the Kentucky Supreme Court to answer a determinative question of Kentucky law when there is no controlling Kentucky precedent.","c":"RAP 50(A)–(B)"}]],["Stays and supersedeas bonds",[{"t":"Supersedeas bond stays a money judgment","d":"Except for a judgment granting a permanent injunction, the appellant may stay enforcement by posting a supersedeas bond, effective when approved by the court or clerk. For an unsecured money judgment, the bond covers the unsatisfied judgment, appeal costs, and interest, unless the trial court sets a different amount or other security for good cause after notice and hearing. The trial court keeps jurisdiction over bond questions during the appeal. Exempt governmental units get an automatic stay on filing the notice of appeal.","c":"RAP 63(A)–(C) (AO 2026-01)"}]],["eFiling in the appellate courts",[{"t":"An eFiled appellate document is only ‘tendered’ until the clerk accepts it","d":"In the appellate courts, eFiling submits a tendered document. The clerk reviews it for conformity before docketing and sends a notice of whether it was accepted. A deficient document must be resent in a new envelope. An untimely document not attached to a motion for enlargement may be rejected. Appellate clerks may post extra technical requirements for briefs.","c":"eFiling Rules § 9 (AO 2025-02)"},{"t":"Deadline and service","d":"eFilings must be completed before midnight in the receiving court’s time zone. Notice of entry sent through the system counts as service of notice under CR 77.04 and RAP 40 for parties who consented to e-service. Parties who have not consented must be served on paper. Electronic service adds 3 days under CR 6.05.","c":"eFiling Rules §§ 8(2)(b), 13(3), 13(7), 14(2) (AO 2025-02)"}]],["What changed on April 1, 2026 (AO 2026-01)",[{"t":"Summary of the 2026 amendments","d":"RAP 2 was rewritten: the fee clause moved to 2(I), and heard-together and consolidated appeals were redefined. RAP 7 motions practice was restated, with a 10-day response and rules for voluntary dismissal. The prehearing statement under RAP 22 became an optional motion. New RAP 23 requires notice to the Attorney General under KRS 418.075 before the appellant’s brief when a statute’s constitutionality is challenged; the AG has 10 days to appear. The reply-brief trigger in RAP 30(C)(1)(c) changed. The Supreme Court combined reply/cross-appellee limit rose to 19,250 words. The statement-of-points threshold became 1,750 words. RAP 60(J)(1) and RAP 63(B)–(C) were also amended.","c":"AO 2026-01"}]],["Deadlines",[{"t":"Notice of appeal (civil)","d":"30 days from clerk’s docket notation of service of the judgment or order (CR 77.04(2)).\nJurisdictional; cannot be extended except up to 10 extra days for excusable neglect in learning of entry (RAP 3(D)).","c":"RAP 3(A)(1)–(2)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (clerk’s docket notation of service of the judgment or order)","note":"Jurisdictional; cannot be extended except up to 10 extra days for excusable neglect in learning of entry (RAP 3(D))."}},{"t":"Extension for failure to learn of entry","d":"10 days from expiration of the original appeal time.\nGranted by the trial court on excusable neglect.","c":"RAP 3(D)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (expiration of the original appeal time)","note":"Granted by the trial court on excusable neglect."}},{"t":"Notice of appeal after a tolling motion","d":"30 days from docket notation of service of the order disposing of the last CR 50.02, 52.02 or 59 motion.\nOther motions, including CR 60.02, do not toll.","c":"RAP 3(E)(2), (4)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (docket notation of service of the order disposing of the last CR 50.02, 52.02 or 59 motion)","note":"Other motions, including CR 60.02, do not toll."}},{"t":"Notice of cross-appeal","d":"10 days from last day allowed for filing the notice of appeal.\nNo extension (RAP 6(C)–(D)).","c":"RAP 4(B)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (last day allowed for filing the notice of appeal)","note":"No extension (RAP 6(C)–(D))."}},{"t":"Additional cross-appeal","d":"10 days from filing of the notice of cross-appeal that first affects the party.","c":"RAP 4(E)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the notice of cross-appeal that first affects the party)","note":""}},{"t":"Motion to transfer to Supreme Court","d":"10 days from filing of notice of appeal to the Court of Appeals.\nNo extension; response 10 days.","c":"RAP 17(A)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (filing of notice of appeal to the Court of Appeals)","note":"No extension; response 10 days."}},{"t":"Pay fee or appeal denial of in forma pauperis","d":"30 days from denial of the in forma pauperis motion.","c":"RAP 3(A)(3)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (denial of the in forma pauperis motion)","note":""}},{"t":"Motion for prehearing conference (Court of Appeals civil)","d":"20 days from notice of appeal (or notice of cross-appeal).\nOptional since April 1, 2026; other parties respond within 10 days (RAP 22(C)(3)).","c":"RAP 22(A)(1) (AO 2026-01)","due":{"n":20,"from":"the trigger date (notice of appeal)","note":"Optional since April 1, 2026; other parties respond within 10 days (RAP 22(C)(3))."}},{"t":"Designation of recordings for the record","d":"10 days from notice of appeal (or order ending prehearing procedure if RAP 22 applies).\nCounter-designation 10 days after (RAP 24(A)(2)).","c":"RAP 24(A)(1)(b)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (notice of appeal)","note":"Counter-designation 10 days after (RAP 24(A)(2))."}},{"t":"Clerk certifies record","d":"30 days from first notice of appeal (or order ending prehearing procedure).\nClerk duty; may be extended on motion.","c":"RAP 26(B)(4)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (first notice of appeal)","note":"Clerk duty; may be extended on motion."}},{"t":"Appellant’s brief","d":"60 days from docket notation of clerk’s notice of certification of the record (or order granting discretionary review).\nExpedited appeals: 30 days (RAP 30(E)).","c":"RAP 30(C)(1)(a)","due":{"n":60,"from":"the trigger date (docket notation of clerk’s notice of certification of the record)","note":"Expedited appeals: 30 days (RAP 30(E))."}},{"t":"Appellee’s brief","d":"60 days from filing of appellant’s brief.\nExpedited: 30 days.","c":"RAP 30(C)(1)(b)","due":{"n":60,"from":"the trigger date (filing of appellant’s brief)","note":"Expedited: 30 days."}},{"t":"Reply brief","d":"15 days from filing of last appellee’s brief (or when due, if replying to several).\nExpedited: 10 days.","c":"RAP 30(C)(1)(c) (AO 2026-01)","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (filing of last appellee’s brief)","note":"Expedited: 10 days."}},{"t":"Combined appellee/cross-appellant brief","d":"60 days from filing of appellant’s brief.","c":"RAP 30(C)(2)(b)","due":{"n":60,"from":"the trigger date (filing of appellant’s brief)","note":""}},{"t":"Combined appellant reply/cross-appellee brief","d":"60 days from last appellee brief filed or due.\nCross-appellant reply 15 days after (RAP 30(C)(2)(d)).","c":"RAP 30(C)(2)(c)","due":{"n":60,"from":"the trigger date (last appellee brief filed or due)","note":"Cross-appellant reply 15 days after (RAP 30(C)(2)(d))."}},{"t":"Amicus motion and brief","d":"15 days from appellant’s brief filed or due, whichever is later.","c":"RAP 34(B)(2)","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (appellant’s brief filed or due, whichever is later)","note":""}},{"t":"Response to a motion","d":"10 days from filing of the motion.","c":"RAP 7(C) (AO 2026-01)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the motion)","note":""}},{"t":"Corrected document after deficiency notice","d":"10 days from date of the deficiency notice.","c":"RAP 5(F)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (date of the deficiency notice)","note":""}},{"t":"Motion for relief from temporary-injunction order","d":"20 days from entry of the circuit court’s interlocutory order.\nResponse 10 days.","c":"RAP 20(B)","due":{"n":20,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the circuit court’s interlocutory order)","note":"Response 10 days."}},{"t":"Supreme Court review of RAP 20/21 ruling","d":"10 days from entry of the Court of Appeals order.\nDiscretionary; extraordinary cause.","c":"RAP 20(F)(1); RAP 21(B)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the Court of Appeals order)","note":"Discretionary; extraordinary cause."}},{"t":"Petition for rehearing, modification, extension or publication","d":"20 days from date the opinion was issued.\nResponse 20 days after petition.","c":"RAP 43(B)(3)","due":{"n":20,"from":"the trigger date (date the opinion was issued)","note":"Response 20 days after petition."}},{"t":"Motion to reconsider an order","d":"20 days from entry of the order.\nNot available for RAP 17, 20, 21 or 44 orders.","c":"RAP 43(D)(1)","due":{"n":20,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the order)","note":"Not available for RAP 17, 20, 21 or 44 orders."}},{"t":"Motion for discretionary review (Supreme Court)","d":"30 days from court of Appeals decision (or denial of, or decision on, rehearing/reconsideration).\nResponse 30 days; no enlargement after the fact (RAP 6(D)).","c":"RAP 44(B)(2)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (court of Appeals decision)","note":"Response 30 days; no enlargement after the fact (RAP 6(D))."}},{"t":"Motion for discretionary review (Court of Appeals, district-court cases)","d":"30 days from entry of the circuit court’s appellate judgment.","c":"RAP 44(B)(1)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the circuit court’s appellate judgment)","note":""}},{"t":"Cross-motion for discretionary review","d":"10 days from order granting the motion for discretionary review.\nResponse 10 days.","c":"RAP 46(A)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (order granting the motion for discretionary review)","note":"Response 10 days."}},{"t":"Statement of appeal (district court to circuit court)","d":"30 days from filing of the notice of appeal.\nCounterstatement 30 days after statement (RAP 48(F)(2)).","c":"RAP 48(F)(1)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the notice of appeal)","note":"Counterstatement 30 days after statement (RAP 48(F)(2))."}},{"t":"Response to original action (writ) petition","d":"20 days from filing of the petition.","c":"RAP 60(D)","due":{"n":20,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the petition)","note":""}},{"t":"Appeal of Court of Appeals final order in original action","d":"30 days from entry of the judgment or order.\nFiled with the Court of Appeals clerk.","c":"RAP 60(I)(2)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the judgment or order)","note":"Filed with the Court of Appeals clerk."}},{"t":"Anti-SLAPP special motion","d":"60 days from service of the pleading asserting the claim.\nLater only for good cause; order is appealable as of right (KRS 454.476).","c":"KRS 454.464","due":{"n":60,"from":"the trigger date (service of the pleading asserting the claim)","note":"Later only for good cause; order is appealable as of right (KRS 454.476)."}},{"t":"Supreme Court opinion becomes final","d":"21 days from rendition.\nAbsent timely RAP 43 petition.","c":"RAP 40(G)(1)","due":{"n":21,"from":"the trigger date (rendition)","note":"Absent timely RAP 43 petition."}},{"t":"Court of Appeals opinion becomes final","d":"31 days from rendition.\nAbsent timely RAP 43 petition or RAP 44 motion.","c":"RAP 40(G)(1)","due":{"n":31,"from":"the trigger date (rendition)","note":"Absent timely RAP 43 petition or RAP 44 motion."}}]]],"courts":[],"links":[["Official site","https://www.kycourts.gov/"]]},"rulesName":"Kentucky Rules of Civil Procedure","rulesSrc":"https://www.kycourts.gov/Courts/Supreme-Court/Pages/Administrative-Orders.aspx","rulesAsof":"as checked November 10, 2026","rulesNote":"Each entry is our summary of the rule, not the rule itself. Tap Read the official text before you rely on it.","rules":[{"n":"1(2)","t":"Scope of the rules","s":"The Civil Rules govern all civil actions in the Court of Justice, with two exceptions: special statutory proceedings, where an inconsistent statutory procedure controls, and appeals, which are governed by the Rules of Appellate Procedure since January 1, 2023. AOC regulations and manuals issued with Supreme Court authorization on internal court policy have the same effect as the Rules.","u":"https://www.kycourts.gov/Courts/Supreme-Court/Pages/Administrative-Orders.aspx"},{"n":"3.02","t":"Circuit Court civil fees and costs","s":"From July 1, 2026, the Circuit Court civil filing fee is $188. There is no fee for habeas corpus, RCr 11.42, or KRS 202A/202B/387 mental-health proceedings, and a KRS 311.732 petition costs $10. A $20 court technology fee and KRS 453.060 and 27A.630 fees are added. Other costs: six-person jury $40; larger jury $70; third-party complaint $44; certification $5; copies $0.25 per page; video copy $25; audio copy $15; attachment, execution or possession writs $25; garnishments $15. Certified-mail service costs $35 from July 1, 2026 (AO 2026-18).","u":"https://www.kycourts.gov/Courts/Supreme-Court/Pages/Administrative-Orders.aspx"},{"n":"3.03","t":"District Court civil fees and costs","s":"From July 1, 2026, the District Court civil filing fee is $94. Small claims (cases of $2,500 or less) cost $38. Cases of $500 or less outside small claims, probate, guardianship or conservatorship applications, name changes, paternity, and forcible detainer each cost $50. A petition to marry under KRS 402 costs $25, and a KRS 311.732 petition $13. There is no fee for KRS 202A/202B/387 mental-health cases or KRS 159.051 student hearings. The same $20 technology fee and additional costs apply as in Circuit Court, with a six-person jury at $40.","u":"https://www.kycourts.gov/Courts/Supreme-Court/Pages/Administrative-Orders.aspx"},{"n":"6.05","t":"Additional time after service by mail","s":"Service by mail adds 3 days to a prescribed period. Electronic service through the eFiling system is treated the same way. (Rule text not collected. Summary taken from the cross-reference cited.) See eFiling Rules § 13(7).","u":"https://www.kycourts.gov/Courts/Supreme-Court/Pages/Administrative-Orders.aspx"},{"n":"50.02, 52.02, 59","t":"Post-judgment motions (judgment notwithstanding the verdict, amended findings, new trial / alter or amend)","s":"A timely motion under any of these rules tolls the time to appeal for all parties until the order disposing of the last such motion. A CR 59 order granting a new trial does not restart the time. Motions under other rules, such as CR 60.02, do not toll. (Rule text not collected. Summary taken from the cross-reference cited.) See RAP 3(E)(2).","u":"https://www.kycourts.gov/Courts/Supreme-Court/Pages/Administrative-Orders.aspx"},{"n":"53.03","t":"Master commissioner: powers","s":"An order of reference or local rule may limit the master commissioner to particular issues or acts and may set hearing times and a report deadline. Within those limits, the commissioner regulates hearings, may require production of books and papers, rules on admissibility unless directed otherwise, administers oaths, and examines witnesses and parties. On request, the commissioner makes a record of excluded evidence as in a nonjury trial. Gender-neutral wording took effect January 1, 2023.","u":"https://www.kycourts.gov/Courts/Supreme-Court/Pages/Administrative-Orders.aspx"},{"n":"53.07","t":"Master commissioner: limit on compensation","s":"A master commissioner’s total personal compensation from fees is capped under the Rules of Administrative Procedure, Part IV, unless the Chief Justice approves more. Fees above compensation and office expenses are remitted under CR 53.08, but six months of anticipated expenses may be kept.","u":"https://www.kycourts.gov/Courts/Supreme-Court/Pages/Administrative-Orders.aspx"},{"n":"54.02(1)","t":"Judgment on fewer than all claims or parties","s":"When a judgment is made final and appealable on fewer than all claims or parties, a notice of appeal brings in only the parties against whom that judgment runs. (Rule text not collected. Summary taken from the cross-reference cited.) See RAP 2(A)(2).","u":"https://www.kycourts.gov/Courts/Supreme-Court/Pages/Administrative-Orders.aspx"},{"n":"62.02","t":"Pending appeal of injunction judgments (deleted)","s":"Deleted effective January 1, 2023. Injunction relief pending appeal is now under RAP 20(C).","u":"https://www.kycourts.gov/Courts/Supreme-Court/Pages/Administrative-Orders.aspx"},{"n":"62.03","t":"Pending appeal of injunction judgments (deleted)","s":"Deleted effective January 1, 2023. See RAP 20 and RAP 63.","u":"https://www.kycourts.gov/Courts/Supreme-Court/Pages/Administrative-Orders.aspx"},{"n":"65.06","t":"Enforcement of restraining orders and injunctions","s":"Compliance with a restraining order or injunction may be compelled, or disobedience punished as contempt, by the judge where the action is pending. If that judge is disqualified or absent from the district, any circuit judge may act, proceeding by rule and attachment on an affidavit or other evidence of breach. An injunction granted by an appellate court under RAP 20 is enforced by that appellate court.","u":"https://www.kycourts.gov/Courts/Supreme-Court/Pages/Administrative-Orders.aspx"},{"n":"65.07","t":"Interlocutory relief in Court of Appeals (deleted)","s":"Deleted effective January 1, 2023. Replaced by RAP 20(B): a motion to the Court of Appeals within 20 days of an order on a temporary injunction.","u":"https://www.kycourts.gov/Courts/Supreme-Court/Pages/Administrative-Orders.aspx"},{"n":"65.08","t":"Interlocutory relief pending appeal from final judgment (deleted)","s":"Deleted effective January 1, 2023. See RAP 20(C) and RAP 21.","u":"https://www.kycourts.gov/Courts/Supreme-Court/Pages/Administrative-Orders.aspx"},{"n":"65.09","t":"Interlocutory relief in the Supreme Court (deleted)","s":"Deleted effective January 1, 2023. See RAP 20(F): a motion within 10 days, discretionary, requiring extraordinary cause.","u":"https://www.kycourts.gov/Courts/Supreme-Court/Pages/Administrative-Orders.aspx"},{"n":"72.01–72.13","t":"Appeals from District Court (deleted)","s":"Deleted effective January 1, 2023. Now RAP 48.","u":"https://www.kycourts.gov/Courts/Supreme-Court/Pages/Administrative-Orders.aspx"},{"n":"73.01–73.08","t":"All appeals (deleted)","s":"Deleted effective January 1, 2023. Now RAP 2–3 and 5–6.","u":"https://www.kycourts.gov/Courts/Supreme-Court/Pages/Administrative-Orders.aspx"},{"n":"74.01–74.02","t":"Cross-appeals (deleted)","s":"Deleted effective January 1, 2023. Now RAP 4.","u":"https://www.kycourts.gov/Courts/Supreme-Court/Pages/Administrative-Orders.aspx"},{"n":"75.01–75.15","t":"Record on appeal (deleted)","s":"Deleted effective January 1, 2023. Now RAP 24–28.","u":"https://www.kycourts.gov/Courts/Supreme-Court/Pages/Administrative-Orders.aspx"},{"n":"76.01–76.46","t":"Practice in Court of Appeals and Supreme Court (deleted)","s":"Deleted effective January 1, 2023. Now RAP 7–63.","u":"https://www.kycourts.gov/Courts/Supreme-Court/Pages/Administrative-Orders.aspx"},{"n":"77.04","t":"Notice of entry of judgments and orders","s":"Immediately on entry of a judgment, a final order, an order affecting appeal time, or an order required to be served, the clerk serves notice of entry by mail under CR 5 on every party not in default, and notes the service date on the docket. That notation date, or the date of an earlier written waiver, is the date of entry for running appeal time under RAP 3. The court must require notice of entry for orders under CR 6.03(2), 12.01, 12.05, 43.04, 50.02, 52.02, 54.02, 59, 62.01, 62.04, RAP 20, and ex parte orders. The appeal trigger is the docket notation of service, not the entry itself. Lack of notice does not extend appeal time, except as RAP 3 permits (up to 10 days).","u":"https://www.kycourts.gov/Courts/Supreme-Court/Pages/Administrative-Orders.aspx"},{"n":"79.05(1)","t":"Original record; removal","s":"The trial clerk keeps all papers in each action, which together with the docket form the original record. The record may leave the clerk’s office only on court order, on transmittal to an appellate court, or on withdrawal by counsel under RAP 26(D). It may be transferred to another county’s circuit clerk on a signed written request by counsel when a hearing is held there, and must be returned promptly afterward.","u":"https://www.kycourts.gov/Courts/Supreme-Court/Pages/Administrative-Orders.aspx"},{"n":"79.06","t":"Clerks of the Court of Appeals and Supreme Court (deleted)","s":"Deleted effective January 1, 2023.","u":"https://www.kycourts.gov/Courts/Supreme-Court/Pages/Administrative-Orders.aspx"},{"n":"98","t":"Video recorded court proceedings and appeals","s":"When a trial judge uses video recording, the official record is two simultaneous recordings, or two copies of the digital recording, labeled by the clerk in a set format. The clerk provides duplicates to counsel for an AOC-set fee. The judge keeps an exhibit list, a trial log, and a witness time log. By pretrial order, photographs may replace physical exhibits. A video deposition or its transcript may be the official record of the deposition. Any party may bring a stenographic reporter.","u":"https://www.kycourts.gov/Courts/Supreme-Court/Pages/Administrative-Orders.aspx"},{"n":"99.01","t":"Mediation: authority, preamble and scope","s":"Adopted effective February 1, 2022, replacing the Model Mediation Rules. CR 99 governs every court-ordered mediation, and parties are encouraged to follow it otherwise. Mediation may not be ordered where a party may pose a risk of harm, or in conflict with KRS 403.036. A court may enforce its order to mediate. It may not reallocate mediation costs afterward or penalize a party because a case settles after mediation. No local rule may conflict.","u":"https://www.kycourts.gov/Courts/Supreme-Court/Pages/Administrative-Orders.aspx"},{"n":"99.02","t":"Mediation defined","s":"Mediation is an informal process in which a neutral mediator helps the parties reach agreement. Decision-making authority stays with the parties.","u":"https://www.kycourts.gov/Courts/Supreme-Court/Pages/Administrative-Orders.aspx"},{"n":"99.03","t":"Referral of cases to mediation","s":"The court may refer a case, or part of one, on its own motion or a party’s, after weighing listed factors: stage of the case and discovery, nature of the issues, value of confidentiality and speed, willingness to settle, prior attempts, ability to participate (including virtually), and cost. Blanket referral of all cases or of a case type is barred, and so is requiring mediation before a trial date is set.","u":"https://www.kycourts.gov/Courts/Supreme-Court/Pages/Administrative-Orders.aspx"},{"n":"99.04","t":"No stay of proceedings","s":"Referral to mediation does not stay discovery or other proceedings unless the court orders a stay or the parties agree in writing.","u":"https://www.kycourts.gov/Courts/Supreme-Court/Pages/Administrative-Orders.aspx"},{"n":"99.05","t":"Appointment of mediator","s":"Within 15 days of referral, the parties must agree on a mediator or mediation service. If they cannot agree, they notify the court, which selects a mediator recognized as trained or experienced in civil actions. This wording took effect January 1, 2023.","u":"https://www.kycourts.gov/Courts/Supreme-Court/Pages/Administrative-Orders.aspx"},{"n":"99.06","t":"Mediator compensation","s":"An agreed mediator is paid the agreed rate. A court-appointed mediator may charge no more than the mediator’s standard rate. The parties split fees equally unless they agree or the court orders otherwise.","u":"https://www.kycourts.gov/Courts/Supreme-Court/Pages/Administrative-Orders.aspx"},{"n":"99.07","t":"Mediation procedure","s":"The conference is held at an agreed time and place, or as the court directs. The mediator may confer with parties beforehand, including ex parte, and may require confidential case statements.","u":"https://www.kycourts.gov/Courts/Supreme-Court/Pages/Administrative-Orders.aspx"},{"n":"99.08","t":"Attendance at mediation conference","s":"Parties and counsel attend unless the court orders or the parties explicitly agree otherwise. A public entity sends a representative with full authority to negotiate and recommend. Other organizations send a non-counsel representative with full settlement authority. Insured parties’ carriers send a representative, not outside counsel, with full authority. ‘Full authority’ does not require authority for any specific amount. A later sentence says that, unless the court expressly orders it, a represented party and the carrier representative need not be present, which is in tension with paragraph 4. Any or all attendance may be virtual unless the parties agree or the court orders otherwise.","u":"https://www.kycourts.gov/Courts/Supreme-Court/Pages/Administrative-Orders.aspx"},{"n":"99.09","t":"Reporting to the court","s":"A court-designated mediator gives written notice if the case is not accepted, and may refer it back for good cause. On settlement, counsel promptly notify the court and submit an order. A partial settlement requires a joint statement of resolved and remaining issues within 10 days after mediation ends. Counsel report in writing when the process ends, and they report only the lack of agreement unless all parties consent to more.","u":"https://www.kycourts.gov/Courts/Supreme-Court/Pages/Administrative-Orders.aspx"},{"n":"99.10","t":"Agreement","s":"Any agreement reached must be put in writing and signed by the parties. The parties draft it, and the mediator may help with their consent.","u":"https://www.kycourts.gov/Courts/Supreme-Court/Pages/Administrative-Orders.aspx"},{"n":"99.11","t":"Confidentiality","s":"Conferences are closed. Recording requires everyone’s agreement. Mediation counts as settlement negotiations under KRE 408. All mediation communications, including those before and after the conference, are privileged, confidential, not discoverable, and inadmissible, but otherwise-admissible evidence does not become protected just because it was used in mediation. The mediator may not disclose to non-participants or court staff, or pass along information given in confidence. The mediator’s privilege belongs to the mediator, cannot be waived by the parties, and yields only to a court order for good cause. Abuse reporting is allowed. A signed settlement is not confidential unless the parties stipulate, and loses confidentiality when needed to enforce it. (CR 99.11(8) as corrected by AO 2022-08.)","u":"https://www.kycourts.gov/Courts/Supreme-Court/Pages/Administrative-Orders.aspx"},{"n":"100.01","t":"Code of Conduct for Mediators: purpose","s":"The Code applies to all mediators in mediations governed by CR 99 and CR 100 (as corrected by AO 2022-08). It sets minimum standards. The mediator facilitates and does not decide the dispute.","u":"https://www.kycourts.gov/Courts/Supreme-Court/Pages/Administrative-Orders.aspx"},{"n":"100.02","t":"Competency","s":"The mediator must obtain appropriate skills and training and keep upgrading them. Process competence matters more than subject-matter expertise.","u":"https://www.kycourts.gov/Courts/Supreme-Court/Pages/Administrative-Orders.aspx"},{"n":"100.03","t":"Impartiality","s":"The mediator must stay impartial. By the first session, the mediator must disclose relationships with parties or counsel, personal knowledge of the facts, or any economic interest. The mediator must withdraw if a party keeps objecting on impartiality grounds after discussion, or if the mediator cannot be impartial.","u":"https://www.kycourts.gov/Courts/Supreme-Court/Pages/Administrative-Orders.aspx"},{"n":"100.04","t":"Confidentiality","s":"The mediator must protect confidentiality as required by CR 99.11, subject to its exceptions.","u":"https://www.kycourts.gov/Courts/Supreme-Court/Pages/Administrative-Orders.aspx"},{"n":"100.05","t":"Consent","s":"The mediator must make reasonable efforts to ensure each party understands the process, the mediator’s role, and their options, and may suggest they get professional advice.","u":"https://www.kycourts.gov/Courts/Supreme-Court/Pages/Administrative-Orders.aspx"},{"n":"100.06","t":"Self-determination","s":"The mediator respects party self-determination, is not directive or judgmental, and may raise questions and suggest options. The mediator may not impose opinions on the merits, but may give evaluative observations on request.","u":"https://www.kycourts.gov/Courts/Supreme-Court/Pages/Administrative-Orders.aspx"},{"n":"100.07","t":"Separation from legal and professional advice","s":"A mediator acts only as mediator and gives no legal or other professional advice during the mediation.","u":"https://www.kycourts.gov/Courts/Supreme-Court/Pages/Administrative-Orders.aspx"},{"n":"100.08","t":"Conflicts of interest","s":"The mediator may not charge contingent or outcome-based fees, use mediation information for personal gain, take on mediations that cannot be delivered timely, or give or receive referral consideration (de minimis refreshments excepted). The mediator should not accept gifts that raise impartiality questions.","u":"https://www.kycourts.gov/Courts/Supreme-Court/Pages/Administrative-Orders.aspx"},{"n":"100.09","t":"Protecting the integrity of the process","s":"The mediator encourages mutual respect and takes reasonable steps, consistent with self-determination, to limit abuse of the process.","u":"https://www.kycourts.gov/Courts/Supreme-Court/Pages/Administrative-Orders.aspx"}]});
